---

<DocsLayout
  title="Running nikcli in CI"
  subtitle="Mention it on a pull request with the GitHub Action, or drive a headless server from any CI with the API."
  description="Guide to running nikcli in CI: the GitHub Action and its inputs, OIDC token exchange, scheduled runs, and driving a headless server over the API from any provider."
  toc={toc}
>
  <section id="two-ways" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Two ways to run in CI</h2>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th></th><th>GitHub Action</th><th>API</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Setup</td><td>One workflow file</td><td>Start a server, write a script</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Triggered by</td><td>A mention in an issue or PR comment</td><td>Anything you can script</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Posts back to GitHub</td><td>Built in</td><td>You do it</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Works on</td><td>GitHub Actions</td><td>Any CI</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Control over the flow</td><td>The action's inputs</td><td>Total</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Start with the action. Move to the API when you need a shape the action does not have — a
      nightly job that opens its own PR, a gate that fails the build, a run on GitLab.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="action" class="doc-section">
    <h2>The GitHub Action</h2>
    <p>
      The action runs nikcli inside a workflow and answers when someone mentions it in a comment.
      The default triggers are <code>/nikcli</code> and <code>/nik</code>.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="yaml" title=".github/workflows/nikcli.yml">{`name: nikcli

on:
  issue_comment:
    types: [created]
  pull_request_review_comment:
    types: [created]

jobs:
  nikcli:
    if: |
      contains(github.event.comment.body, '/nik') ||
      contains(github.event.comment.body, '/nikcli')
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    permissions:
      id-token: write
    steps:
      - name: Checkout repository
        uses: actions/checkout@v6
        with:
          fetch-depth: 1
          persist-credentials: false

      - name: Run nikcli
        uses: nikomatt69/nikcli/github@latest
        env:
          ANTHROPIC_API_KEY: \${{ secrets.ANTHROPIC_API_KEY }}
          GITHUB_TOKEN: \${{ secrets.GITHUB_TOKEN }}
        with:
          model: anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-20250514
          use_github_token: true`}</DocCode>
    <p>
      Provider keys go in repository or organisation secrets — <strong>Settings → Secrets and
      variables → Actions</strong>. The <code>if:</code> guard matters: without it the job starts on
      every comment and exits, which is slow and noisy.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="inputs" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Action inputs</h2>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Input</th><th>Required</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>model</code></td><td>yes</td><td>The model to run, as <code>provider/model</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>agent</code></td><td>no</td><td>A primary agent. Falls back to the configured default, then <code>build</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>prompt</code></td><td>no</td><td>Overrides the default prompt — this is how a scheduled job says what to do.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>mentions</code></td><td>no</td><td>Comma-separated trigger phrases, case-insensitive. Defaults to <code>/nikcli,/nik</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>share</code></td><td>no</td><td>Share the session. Defaults to true on public repositories.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>variant</code></td><td>no</td><td>Provider-specific reasoning effort, such as <code>high</code> or <code>minimal</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>use_github_token</code></td><td>no</td><td>Use <code>GITHUB_TOKEN</code> directly and skip OIDC exchange. Defaults to <code>false</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>oidc_base_url</code></td><td>no</td><td>Only for a custom GitHub App install. Defaults to <code>https://api.nikcli.store</code>.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <DocCallout type="warn" title="share defaults to true on public repos">
      <p>
        A shared session gets a public link. That is usually the point on an open-source repository
        and usually not what you want on a private one that has been made public — set
        <code>share: false</code> explicitly if you are unsure.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="tokens" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Token exchange vs GITHUB_TOKEN</h2>
    <p>
      By default the action exchanges a GitHub OIDC token for an app token, which is why the job
      needs <code>permissions: id-token: write</code>. Setting
      <code>use_github_token: true</code> skips that and uses the workflow's own
      <code>GITHUB_TOKEN</code>.
    </p>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th></th><th>OIDC exchange (default)</th><th><code>use_github_token: true</code></th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Needs</td><td><code>id-token: write</code> and the app installed</td><td>Only <code>GITHUB_TOKEN</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Comments appear as</td><td>The nikcli app</td><td><code>github-actions[bot]</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Good for</td><td>Normal use</td><td>Forks, self-hosted, no app install</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <DocCallout type="info" title="A bare Transport error means the child died">
      <p>
        If a run fails with nothing but <code>##[error]Transport</code>, the nikcli server the
        action started exited — the transport error is the symptom, not the cause. Look above it in
        the log for the child's own output.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="schedule" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Running it on a schedule</h2>
    <p>
      The action does not have to be triggered by a comment. Give it a <code>prompt</code> and a
      <code>schedule</code> trigger and it becomes an unattended job.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="yaml" title=".github/workflows/nightly.yml">{`name: nightly-audit

on:
  schedule:
    - cron: "0 3 * * *"
  workflow_dispatch:

jobs:
  audit:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    permissions:
      id-token: write
      contents: read
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v6
        with:
          persist-credentials: false

      - uses: nikomatt69/nikcli/github@latest
        env:
          ANTHROPIC_API_KEY: \${{ secrets.ANTHROPIC_API_KEY }}
          GITHUB_TOKEN: \${{ secrets.GITHUB_TOKEN }}
        with:
          model: anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-20250514
          agent: plan
          share: "false"
          prompt: |
            Audit the repository for dependencies more than two majors behind.
            Report findings only. Do not modify any files.`}</DocCode>
    <DocCallout type="note" title="Say what it must not do">
      <p>
        An unattended run has nobody to stop it. "Report findings only. Do not modify any files" is
        doing as much work in that prompt as the instruction itself.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="api" class="doc-section">
    <h2>The API path</h2>
    <p>
      On any other CI, start a server in the job and drive it. The server is local to the runner, so
      loopback needs no password.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="yaml" title=".gitlab-ci.yml">{`review:
  image: oven/bun:1
  script:
    - bun add -g nikcli-ai
    - nikcli serve --hostname 127.0.0.1 --port 4096 &
    - until curl -sf http://127.0.0.1:4096/global/health; do sleep 1; done
    - bun run ci/review.ts
  variables:
    ANTHROPIC_API_KEY: $ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`}</DocCode>
    <DocCallout type="warn" title="Wait for health, do not sleep">
      <p>
        The server picks an ephemeral port if the requested one is taken, and starts in variable
        time. Poll <code>/global/health</code> rather than guessing a sleep — a fixed sleep is the
        single most common cause of a flaky nikcli CI job.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="permissions" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Permissions in an unattended run</h2>
    <p>
      Nothing is watching, so a permission request stops the run forever unless something answers
      it. Answer deliberately rather than approving everything.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="ts">{`const ALLOWED = new Set(["read", "grep", "glob", "webfetch"])

const pump = setInterval(async () => {
  const { data: pending } = await client.permission.list()
  for (const request of pending ?? []) {
    await client.permission.reply({
      requestID: request.id,
      reply: ALLOWED.has(request.permission) ? "once" : "reject",
    })
  }
}, 1000)`}</DocCode>
    <p>
      A throwaway container is the one place blanket approval is defensible. A runner with
      deployment credentials is not — see <a href="/docs/permissions">Permissions</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="complete" class="doc-section">
    <h2>A review job on any CI</h2>
    <p>
      Reviews the diff against the base branch, prints the findings, and fails the build if the
      agent reports something blocking.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="ts" title="ci/review.ts">{`import { createNikcliClient } from "@nikcli-ai/sdk/httpapi"

const client = createNikcliClient({
  baseUrl: "http://127.0.0.1:4096",
  directory: process.cwd(),
})

const { data: session, error } = await client.session.create({ title: "ci review" })
if (!session) throw error

const READ_ONLY = new Set(["read", "grep", "glob", "list"])
const pump = setInterval(async () => {
  const { data: pending } = await client.permission.list()
  for (const request of pending ?? []) {
    await client.permission.reply({
      requestID: request.id,
      reply: READ_ONLY.has(request.permission) ? "once" : "reject",
    })
  }
}, 1000)

try {
  const base = process.env.CI_MERGE_REQUEST_TARGET_BRANCH_NAME ?? "main"

  const { data: reply } = await client.session.prompt({
    sessionID: session.id,
    parts: [{
      type: "text",
      text: [
        \`Review the diff between HEAD and \${base}.\`,
        "Report correctness bugs only — not style, not naming.",
        "You have read-only access; do not attempt to edit files.",
        "End your reply with the single line BLOCKING: yes or BLOCKING: no.",
      ].join("\\n"),
    }],
  })

  const text = (reply?.parts ?? [])
    .filter((part) => part.type === "text")
    .map((part) => part.text)
    .join("\\n")

  console.log(text)

  if (/^BLOCKING:\\s*yes/im.test(text)) {
    console.error("\\nReview found blocking issues.")
    process.exit(1)
  }
} finally {
  clearInterval(pump)
}`}</DocCode>
    <DocCallout type="info" title="The sentinel line is load-bearing">
      <p>
        Parsing prose for a verdict is guesswork. Asking for one exact line and matching on it is
        the difference between a gate that works and one that fails the build on the word
        "blocking" appearing in a sentence.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="failure-modes" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Failure modes</h2>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Cause</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Job hangs, no output</td><td>A permission request nobody answered.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>##[error]Transport</code> alone</td><td>The nikcli child process died; the real error is earlier in the log.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Connection refused</td><td>Started the script before the server was healthy, or it fell back to another port.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Action never triggers</td><td>The <code>if:</code> guard does not match the mention, or <code>mentions</code> was changed.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>OIDC exchange fails</td><td>Missing <code>permissions: id-token: write</code>, or the app is not installed.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Agent edits files in a read-only job</td><td>Approving every permission instead of filtering by <code>request.permission</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Gate never fails</td><td>Matching prose instead of a sentinel line.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

